cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use AgentKillChain in academic, policy, or safety evaluation work, please cite it using this metadata."
title: "AgentKillChain: A Versioned Benchmark for Persistent Compromise in Tool-Using AI Agents"
authors:
  - family-names: "O'Connor"
    given-names: "Kevin"
    affiliation: "TKOResearch LLC"
repository-code: "https://github.com/KevinOBytes/AgentKillChain"
url: "https://github.com/KevinOBytes/AgentKillChain"
license: "MIT"
date-released: "2026-06-03"
version: "2.3.0"
keywords:
  - "AI safety"
  - "agent evaluation"
  - "prompt injection"
  - "benchmark"
  - "empirical evaluation"
  - "frontier model evaluation"
  - "RAG poisoning"
  - "toolchain confusion"
  - "temperature zero"
  - "LLM-as-judge"
  - "judge calibration"
  - "few-shot rubrics"
abstract: >-
  AgentKillChain is an open, versioned benchmark for evaluating prompt
  injection and persistent compromise in tool-using AI agents. Version 2.3
  features a fully empirical severity-recalibrated catalog (51 scenarios
  across 8 families), improved LLM-as-judge evaluation with few-shot exemplified
  rubrics (gpt-5.4 judge), structured JSON output, and a 552-evaluation live
  run across eleven models spanning the parameter spectrum (GPT-5-pro,
  Claude Opus-4.7, Gemini 3.5 Flash, DeepSeek V4 Pro, Llama-4 Maverick,
  GPT-5-mini, DeepSeek V4 Flash, Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview, O3-mini,
  MIMO-v2.5-pro, GLM-5.1). All proportions report 95% Wilson score confidence
  intervals. Cross-judge validation (refusal κ = 0.492, compliance κ = 0.316)
  meets the 0.4 defensibility threshold for refusal. All severity labels are
  grounded in measured model compliance, not synthetic harm estimates. The v2.3
  judge recalibration reduced compliance detection from 10.0% to 3.4%,
  eliminating false positives from passive acknowledgment.
